# Forms

Most apps accept input in a request body: a JSON document from an API client
or a script in the page, or the fields of an HTML form posted by the
browser. Either way the work is the same three steps. Read the body, turn it
into a typed value, and check that value before anything else touches it.

This page covers both kinds of body, then the validation they share. The
running example is a signup with a name, an email address and an age:

```gleam
pub type SignupInput {
  SignupInput(name: String, email: String, age: Int)
}
```

## JSON bodies

`body.json` reads the request body and decodes it with a decoder from
`gleam/dynamic/decode`. Like the functions in `howdy/query`, it is designed
for `use`: the rest of the handler only runs with a decoded value.

```gleam
import gleam/dynamic/decode
import gleam/json
import howdy/body
import howdy/controller.{type Context}

pub fn signup_controller() {
  controller.new("signups")
  |> controller.post("/", create_signup)
}

fn signup_decoder() -> decode.Decoder(SignupInput) {
  use name <- decode.field("name", decode.string)
  use email <- decode.field("email", decode.string)
  use age <- decode.field("age", decode.int)
  decode.success(SignupInput(name:, email:, age:))
}

fn create_signup(ctx: Context) {
  use input <- body.json(ctx, signup_decoder())
  controller.json(ctx, json.object([#("name", json.string(input.name))]))
  |> controller.with_status(201)
}
```

When the body cannot be decoded, the handler stops and the client gets a
`400` with a JSON body that says why:

- A body that is not JSON answers
  `{"error":"request body is not valid JSON"}`.
- A value of the wrong type, such as `"age":"old"`, answers
  `{"error":"invalid request body: age expected Int, found String"}`.
- Missing fields are all reported together:
  `{"error":"invalid request body: email expected Field, found Nothing; age expected Field, found Nothing"}`.

`body.json` reads at most `body.default_limit` bytes, which is one mebibyte.
A larger body answers `400` with `{"error":"request body too large"}`. Use
`body.json_with_limit` to pick a different limit for one route, such as a
bulk import:

```gleam
use input <- body.json_with_limit(ctx, 10_000_000, import_decoder())
```

The `content-type` header is not checked, so a client that forgets to send
`application/json` is still understood.

For a body too large to hold in memory, such as a file a client sends as
the whole body of a `PUT`, `controller.body_reader(ctx, limit:)` reads it a
piece at a time: call `context.read_chunk` until it gives `None`, writing
each piece wherever it goes.

## HTML forms

A browser posting a `<form>` sends the fields as
`application/x-www-form-urlencoded`: the same `name=Ada&age=36` format as a
query string, but in the body. A form with `enctype="multipart/form-data"`,
which a file input needs, sends each field as a separate part instead.
`howdy/form` reads both. `form.read` is designed for `use` like `body.json`,
and hands the handler a `Form`:

```gleam
import howdy/form

fn create_signup(ctx: Context) {
  use fields <- form.read(ctx)
  controller.text(ctx, "Hello, " <> form.value(fields, "name"))
}
```

A request body can only be read once, so `read` turns it into a value and
every field is then taken from that. Call the value something other than
`form`, such as `fields`: a local variable named `form` would shadow the
module.

The fields arrive already decoded, so
`name=Ada+Lovelace&email=ada%40example.com` gives `"Ada Lovelace"` and
`"ada@example.com"`. When the body cannot be read, the handler stops and the
client gets an error:

- Any other content type answers `415` with
  `{"error":"content type must be application/x-www-form-urlencoded or multipart/form-data"}`.
  Unlike `body.json`, the content type is checked.
- A body with invalid encoding answers `400` with
  `{"error":"request body is not a valid urlencoded form"}`, or a message
  naming what is wrong with a multipart one.
- Fields over `body.default_limit` answer `400` with
  `{"error":"request body too large"}`. `form.read_with_limit(ctx, limit)`
  picks a different limit for one route.
- A file answers `400` with `{"error":"this form does not accept files"}`.
  `form.read` takes no files; see [File uploads](#File-uploads).

### Typed fields

Every form value is a string, so a form needs one more step than JSON:
turning the strings into a typed input. `howdy/form` has a function for each
kind of field. Each takes the form, the field's name and a list of rules
from `howdy/validate`, which is covered [below](#Validation), and they
chain with `use` to build the value:

```gleam
import howdy/form.{type Form}
import howdy/validate

fn signup_input_from_form(fields: Form) -> validate.Result(SignupInput) {
  use name <- form.string(fields, "name", [])
  use email <- form.string(fields, "email", [])
  use age <- form.int(fields, "age", [])
  validate.ok(SignupInput(name:, email:, age:))
}
```

- `string` and `int` require the field. One that is missing reports
  `is required`, and an `int` that is not a whole number reports
  `must be an integer`.
- `optional_string` and `optional_int` give an `Option`, and only run their
  rules when there is a value.
- `checkbox` gives a `Bool`. Browsers leave an unticked checkbox out of the
  submission entirely, so it is `True` when the field is there at all.
- `strings` gives every value of a field that may repeat, such as a
  multi-select or a group of checkboxes sharing a name, in the order sent.

Browsers submit an empty input as `name=`, not as a missing field, and the
helpers differ in what they make of it. `string` keeps the empty string, so
pair it with `validate.not_empty()`. `int` treats it as missing. The
`optional_*` helpers treat it as `None`, as `optional_string` does for a
value its rules leave empty, such as by trimming.

`string`, `int` and the `optional_*` helpers expect their field once. One
submitted twice reports `must occur only once`, so a request cannot slip a
second value past the one you checked.

For anything the helpers do not cover there is raw access. `form.get` gives
the first value of a field as a `Result`, `form.all` gives every value,
`form.value` gives the first value or `""`, and `form.fields` gives every
name and value pair in the order submitted.

### File uploads

A form only takes files when you say how many and how large, so a route
that does not expect uploads cannot be made to fill your disk. Build
limits with `form.limits()` and pass them to `form.read_with`:

```html
<form method="post" action="/avatar" enctype="multipart/form-data">
  <input type="file" name="avatar" accept="image/png,image/jpeg">
  <button>Upload</button>
</form>
```

```gleam
import simplifile

fn change_avatar(ctx: Context) {
  let limits =
    form.limits() |> form.accept_files(max_files: 1, max_file_size: 5_000_000)
  use fields <- form.read_with(ctx, limits)
  let avatar = {
    use avatar <- form.file(fields, "avatar", [
      form.file_type(["image/png", "image/jpeg"]),
    ])
    validate.ok(avatar)
  }
  case avatar {
    Ok(avatar) -> {
      let assert Ok(Nil) = simplifile.copy_file(avatar.path, avatar_path(ctx))
      controller.status(ctx, 303)
      |> response.set_header("location", "/profile")
    }
    Error(errors) ->
      controller.html(ctx, avatar_page(errors))
      |> controller.with_status(422)
  }
}
```

Each file is an `UploadedFile` with its `file_name`, `content_type`, `size`
in bytes and the `path` of a temporary file. Files are written to disk as
they arrive, never held in memory, and deleted once the handler returns,
even if it crashes. Copy or move a file somewhere lasting before then.

- `file` requires one file, `optional_file` gives an `Option`, and `files`
  gives every file of an input with `multiple`. Their rules run on each
  file: `form.max_size(bytes)` and `form.file_type(types)`, which takes
  exact types or patterns such as `"image/*"`.
- A file input left empty is not a file, so `optional_file` gives `None`.
- `form.get_file`, `form.all_files` and `form.uploaded_files` are the raw
  access for files. `form.fields` holds only the text fields.

The limits are a backstop, not validation. A file over `max_file_size`, one
too many, or files over `form.max_total_file_size` stop reading and answer
`400` straight away. For a message beside the input, give the rule a
smaller size than the limit. `form.max_fields_size` limits everything but
file contents, and `form.temporary_directory` picks where files go while a
request uses them, `TMPDIR` by default.

To keep files somewhere lasting, such as an S3 bucket, see
[Howdy Storage](/docs/storage/overview).

The name and content type are whatever the browser claimed. Check the
content itself before relying on the type, and never build a path from the
name; `file_name` has had any directories removed, but that is all.

## Validation

Decoding proves the input has the right shape: `age` is an `Int`. It says
nothing about whether the values make sense: an `age` of `-4` decodes
perfectly well. Checking that is the job of `howdy/validate`.

A validator is a function from the decoded input to a `validate.Result`.
Each field is passed through a list of rules, and the last line builds the
validated value:

```gleam
import howdy/validate

pub type Signup {
  Signup(name: String, email: String, age: Int)
}

fn validate_signup(input: SignupInput) -> validate.Result(Signup) {
  use name <- validate.field("name", input.name, [
    validate.trim(),
    validate.not_empty(),
    validate.max_length(50),
  ])
  use email <- validate.field("email", input.email, [
    validate.trim(),
    validate.email(),
  ])
  use age <- validate.field("age", input.age, [validate.min(13)])
  validate.ok(Signup(name:, email:, age:))
}
```

Returning a second type is the point. A `SignupInput` is whatever the client
sent; a `Signup` can only be made by the validator. Write the rest of your
app to take a `Signup` and the compiler makes sure nothing unchecked gets
through.

Two things are worth knowing about how fields are checked:

- Rules run in order and the first failure is that field's error, so a field
  reports one problem at a time. Rules can also tidy a value: `trim` never
  fails, it hands the trimmed text to the rules after it, and the trimmed
  text is what you get back. Put it first.
- Every field is checked even when an earlier one has failed, so the client
  hears about all the problems at once, not one per attempt.

### The rules

- `trim()` removes leading and trailing whitespace. It never fails.
- `not_empty()` rejects `""`.
- `min_length(n)` and `max_length(n)` bound the length of a string, counted
  in characters.
- `email()` is a light check: something before a single `@`, and a dot in
  the part after it. Only sending a message proves an address is real.
- `min(n)` and `max(n)` bound an `Int`.
- `one_of(allowed, show)` requires one of a list of values. `show` turns a
  value into text for the error message, so for strings pass a function that
  returns its argument.
- `custom(check, message)` builds a rule from your own test.

A custom rule is a function that returns `True` for a good value, plus the
problem to report otherwise. Rules report a
[`Problem`](/docs/i18n/overview#validation-messages), not a sentence, so the
words can be chosen in the reader's language; for a rule of your own, use
`Custom` with a short code for clients and the message:

```gleam
import gleam/string
import howdy/problem

fn no_spaces() -> validate.Rule(String) {
  validate.custom(
    fn(value) { !string.contains(value, " ") },
    problem.Custom("spaces", "must not contain spaces"),
  )
}
```

Messages read best when they finish the sentence "this field...", as the
built-in ones do: "must not be empty", "must be at least 13".

### Validating JSON

`body.validated` is `body.json` with a validator attached. It decodes, then
validates, and the handler only runs with the validated value:

```gleam
fn create_signup(ctx: Context) {
  use signup <- body.validated(ctx, signup_decoder(), validate_signup)
  controller.json(ctx, json.object([#("name", json.string(signup.name))]))
  |> controller.with_status(201)
}
```

Decode failures still answer `400`. Validation failures answer `422` with
every field error:

```json
{"error":"validation failed","code":"validation","fields":[{"field":"name","code":"blank","message":"must not be empty"},{"field":"email","code":"email","message":"must be a valid email address"},{"field":"age","code":"too_small","message":"must be at least 13","minimum":13}]}
```

The two statuses mean different things to a client. A `400` says the request
was malformed, which is a bug in the client. A `422` says the request was
understood but the values were not acceptable, which is something to show
the person filling in the form. The `fields` list is made for that: match
each `field` to an input and show its `message` beside it, or the words for
its `code` in the reader's language.

If you have a value from somewhere other than a JSON body,
`validate.check(ctx, result, next)` does the same job for any
`validate.Result`.

### Validating HTML forms

The field helpers take the same rules as `validate.field`, so a form does
not need a separate input type and validator. Give each field its rules and
build the validated value directly:

```gleam
fn signup_from_form(fields: Form) -> validate.Result(Signup) {
  use name <- form.string(fields, "name", [
    validate.trim(),
    validate.not_empty(),
    validate.max_length(50),
  ])
  use email <- form.string(fields, "email", [
    validate.trim(),
    validate.email(),
  ])
  use age <- form.int(fields, "age", [validate.min(13)])
  validate.ok(Signup(name:, email:, age:))
}
```

As with `validate.field`, every field is checked even when an earlier one
has failed, so all the errors come back together.

Field problems are returned as errors, not sent as responses, because a
browser form wants a page back, not JSON. Call the validator yourself and
decide what to render:

```gleam
fn create_signup(ctx: Context) {
  use fields <- form.read(ctx)
  case signup_from_form(fields) {
    Ok(signup) -> {
      save(signup)
      controller.status(ctx, 303)
      |> response.set_header("location", "/welcome")
    }
    Error(errors) ->
      controller.html(ctx, signup_page(fields, errors))
      |> controller.with_status(422)
  }
}
```

On success, redirect with `303`. The browser follows it with a `GET`, so
refreshing the next page does not post the form a second time.

On failure, render the form again with status `422`, passing in what the
visitor typed and the errors. Two functions do the lookups for one input:
`form.value(fields, "email")` is what was submitted, or `""`, and
`form.error(errors, "email")` is that field's problem, if it has one.
`problem.message` puts a problem into words: `problem.english()` gives
English, and `problem.translated(locale)` the words for the request's
[locale](/docs/i18n/overview).

```gleam
import gleam/option.{None, Some}
import howdy/problem
import lustre/attribute
import lustre/element/html

fn email_input(fields: Form, errors: List(validate.FieldError)) {
  html.label([], [
    html.text("Email"),
    html.input([
      attribute.type_("email"),
      attribute.name("email"),
      attribute.value(form.value(fields, "email")),
    ]),
    case form.error(errors, "email") {
      Some(found) ->
        html.p([attribute.class("error")], [
          html.text(problem.message(found, problem.english())),
        ])
      None -> html.text("")
    },
  ])
}
```

Putting the typed values back into the form saves the visitor from starting
again. They are untrusted text, so build the page with something that
escapes them, such as Lustre's `attribute.value`, not by joining strings.

For a form endpoint that is not a page, such as one posted by a script,
`form.validated(ctx, signup_from_form)` is the form's `body.validated`: a
validation failure answers the same JSON `422`, and the handler only runs
with the validated value.

If an API route and a browser form accept the same thing, keep the rules in
one place. Build a `SignupInput` from the form with no rules, then pass it
through the validator the JSON route uses:

```gleam
signup_input_from_form(fields) |> result.try(validate_signup)
```

### Cross-site requests

A form post is the one kind of write a page on another site can make a
visitor's browser send to you, with their cookies attached and no CORS
preflight in the way. If your forms act for a signed-in visitor, protect
them with the [CSRF middleware](/docs/middleware/csrf).

## Testing forms

`testing.post` sends a JSON body, and `testing.field_errors` decodes a `422`
response back into a list of field errors:

```gleam
import gleam/json
import howdy/problem
import howdy/service.{FieldError}
import howdy/testing

pub fn signup_rejects_children_test() {
  let res =
    testing.post(
      "/signups",
      json.object([
        #("name", json.string("Ada")),
        #("email", json.string("ada@example.com")),
        #("age", json.int(9)),
      ]),
    )
    |> testing.send(app())

  assert res.status == 422
  assert testing.field_errors(res)
    == Ok([FieldError("age", problem.TooSmall(13))])
}
```

For a `400`, `testing.error(res)` gives you the message.

`testing.post_form` posts an HTML form as a browser would. It takes the
fields as name and value pairs, encodes them, and sets the content type:

```gleam
pub fn signup_form_redirects_test() {
  let res =
    testing.post_form("/signups", [
      #("name", "Ada Lovelace"),
      #("email", "ada@example.com"),
      #("age", "36"),
    ])
    |> testing.send(app())

  assert res.status == 303
}
```

Repeat a name to send a field more than once, and leave a checkbox out to
send it unticked. `testing.form_body(pairs)` sets the same body on a request
built another way, such as a `PUT`. If the app uses the
[CSRF middleware](/docs/middleware/csrf), add an `origin` header as well, or
the post answers `403`.

A validator is a plain function, so its rules can also be tested directly,
with no request at all:

```gleam
pub fn name_is_trimmed_test() {
  let input = SignupInput(name: "  Ada ", email: "ada@example.com", age: 36)
  let assert Ok(signup) = validate_signup(input)
  assert signup.name == "Ada"
}
```

`testing.post_multipart` posts a form with files. It takes the fields and a
list of `testing.FormFile`s, and the handler reads real temporary files:

```gleam
pub fn avatar_upload_test() {
  let res =
    testing.post_multipart("/avatar", [], [
      testing.FormFile("avatar", "me.png", "image/png", <<137, 80, 78, 71>>),
    ])
    |> testing.send(app())

  assert res.status == 303
}
```

The same goes for one that takes a `Form`. `form.from_fields` makes a form
from name and value pairs:

```gleam
pub fn age_must_be_a_number_test() {
  let fields =
    form.from_fields([
      #("name", "Ada"),
      #("email", "ada@example.com"),
      #("age", "old"),
    ])

  assert signup_from_form(fields)
    == Error([FieldError("age", problem.NotInteger)])
}
```

`form.with_files` adds files to such a form, each an `UploadedFile` you
build yourself, so a validator's file rules need no request either.
